{
"page": "Contributing",
"section": "Reporting a bug",
"url": "about/contributing.html#reporting-a-bug",
"text": "Open an issue at github.com/PathoGenOmics-Lab/AMAP/issues with the command you ran, the failing task's .command.err, and both software_versions.yml and params.yml from pipeline_info/. Those last two pin the exact versions and parameters, which is usually what the answer turns on. Troubleshooting covers the common cases first."
},
{
"page": "Contributing",
"section": "Working on the code",
"url": "about/contributing.html#working-on-the-code",
"text": "git clone https://github.com/PathoGenOmics-Lab/AMAP cd AMAP pip install -r requirements-test.txt python -m pytest tests/ -v requirements-test.txt covers everything bin/ imports, not only what the tests import directly, and CI installs from the same file. A shorter list appears to work until a test touches classify_cases.py, which pulls in plotly through classify/plotting.py. The suite covers ID handling, overlap arithmetic, case classification, the rules that resolve and recover genes, and the parsers, and it runs in about a second. Anything touching bin/classify/ should come with a test, sinc"
},
{
"page": "Contributing",
"section": "Annotating H37Rv with itself",
"url": "about/contributing.html#annotating-h37rv-with-itself",
"text": "A change to the correction algorithm should also pass the self-test, which has exactly one right answer: annotating H37Rv against its own reference must give back every CDS of the AL123456.3 GenBank record, at exactly its coordinates and strand, under its own name. docker run --rm --platform linux/amd64 -v \"$PWD:/amap\" -w /amap \\     paururo/amap:latest bash tests/selftest/h37rv_selftest.sh GenBank CDS: 4031   AMAP genes: 4031   exact and named right: 4031   not at exactly their coordinates: 0   named wrong: 0   AMAP genes that are no GenBank CDS: 0 The first run spends a couple of minutes on "
},
{
"page": "Contributing",
"section": "Pipeline code and -resume",
"url": "about/contributing.html#pipeline-code-and--resume",
"text": "Nextflow hashes a task's inputs and script, not the files the script calls by path, so python3 ${projectDir}/bin/check_cds.py alone would let -resume hand back what the old check_cds.py produced. Every process that runs AMAP code therefore lists those files in ext code, and its script names task.ext.code in a comment, which puts them in the cache key (Nextflow 24.04 and later): ext code: { files(\"${projectDir}/bin/{check_cds.py,deduplicate_rvs.py,classify/*.py}\") } A script that imports classify needs classify/*.py in the list. The reverse also bites: Nextflow adds to a task's cache key any bi"
},
{
"page": "Contributing",
"section": "Working on the container",
"url": "about/contributing.html#working-on-the-container",
"text": "docker build --platform linux/amd64 -f docker/Dockerfile -t amap:dev . docker/smoke_test.sh amap:dev The smoke test checks the tools, the Python and R libraries, the bundled reference data, and runs the test suite with the image interpreter. Worth doing before proposing a change to the Dockerfile, since a library update can break the pipeline scripts without the image looking any different."
},
{
"page": "Contributing",
"section": "Working on these docs",
"url": "about/contributing.html#working-on-these-docs",
"text": "The website is plain HTML in docs/, with nothing to build on the server: GitHub Pages serves the folder as it is (docs/.nojekyll keeps it from running Jekyll over the markdown), and it opens from a clone too. The long pages are the markdown files, which GitHub also renders, and tools/build_docs.py writes each one's .html next to it, with the site's header, page list, contents and search. python3 tools/build_docs.py          # after editing any .md under docs/ python3 tools/check_site.py          # what CI runs python3 -m http.server -d docs 8000  # then open http://localhost:8000 The front pag"
},
{
"page": "Contributing",
"section": "Style",
"url": "about/contributing.html#style",
"text": "Keep to what is already there: short paragraphs, GitHub's alerts (> [!WARNING]) for the things that actually bite, and concrete numbers rather than \"large\" or \"slow\". Where a threshold appears in the docs it should match the constant in the code, so if you change one, change the other; check_site.py holds the ones it knows to the code."
}
